# Theming and custom tokens

> Every token is a --pu-* CSS variable in cascade layers. Switch theme, contrast and density, apply a bank brand with createTheme, and support RTL.

URL: https://design.punji.app/get-started/theming/
Section: Get started (get-started)

Components read --pu-* CSS variables, never literals, and all of them are in cascade layers. Four colour themes (light, dark and a high-contrast pair), three densities and right-to-left layout are attributes on any element; a brand is a typed `createTheme` call whose contrast is checked when it is generated. Try every mode below.

## Tokens

The tokens live in `@punji/ui/tokens.css`, inside the `punji.tokens` layer: colours (`--pu-color-*`), control heights, radii, spacing, type, motion and shadows. A token that refers to another keeps the reference, so overriding `--pu-color-accent` also moves what is built on it. The full table is [docs/tokens.md](https://github.com/20was/punji-ui/blob/main/docs/tokens.md); the [Colour tokens](/foundations/colour-tokens/) page shows them live.

## Layers: why your CSS wins

The stylesheet declares `@layer punji.tokens, punji.components;`. Tokens are in the first layer and every component rule is in the second. CSS outside any layer always beats layered CSS, whatever the specificity, so a plain rule of yours overrides a Punji rule with no `!important` and no selector arms race.

Layers you declare yourself are ordered by where they are first declared. Declare them before you import the Punji stylesheet if you need a particular order (see [Tailwind interop](/get-started/tailwind/)).

## Light, dark and the system setting

With no attribute, the theme follows `prefers-color-scheme`. `data-theme="light"` or `data-theme="dark"` on any element forces that theme for the element and everything inside it, so a page can be dark while one panel is light. For a user setting, set the attribute on `<html>` and remove it for "system".

A light, dark and system switch:

```
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { SegmentedControl } from '@punji/ui/controls';
import { useLocalStorage } from '@punji/ui/hooks';

type ThemeChoice = 'system' | 'light' | 'dark';

const isTheme = (value: unknown): value is ThemeChoice => value === 'system' || value === 'light' || value === 'dark';
const options = { defaultValue: 'system' as ThemeChoice, validate: isTheme };

/** A light / dark / system switch. "system" removes the attribute, so prefers-color-scheme decides. */
export function ThemeSwitch() {
  const { value, set } = useLocalStorage<ThemeChoice>('app-theme', options);
  useEffect(() => {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    if (value === 'system') root.removeAttribute('data-theme');
    else root.setAttribute('data-theme', value);
  }, [value]);
  return (
    <SegmentedControl<ThemeChoice>
      label="Theme"
      prefix="theme"
      size="sm"
      value={value}
      onChange={set}
      options={[
        { value: 'light', label: 'Light' },
        { value: 'dark', label: 'Dark' },
        { value: 'system', label: 'Auto' },
      ]}
    />
  );
}
```

## High contrast

Import `@punji/ui/styles.css` (or add `@punji/ui/css/contrast.css` to the granular files) and set `data-theme="light-hc"` or `data-theme="dark-hc"`. Both meet WCAG 2.2 AAA: 7 to 1 for every text token on every surface, and 4.5 to 1 for control borders and chart colours. With no `data-theme`, the page switches to them by itself when the system asks for more contrast (`prefers-contrast: more`).

Windows High Contrast (`forced-colors: active`) replaces the palette with system colours. The components redraw what would vanish: selected tabs, chips and options use `Highlight`, focus is an outline, checkboxes and switches are redrawn, loss bars are hatched and gain bars solid, up candles are hollow and down candles filled, and extra chart series are dashed. Gain and loss always carry a sign and an arrow, so nothing depends on colour. The ratios are gated by `scripts/check-contrast.mjs` in `npm run lint`; the details are on [Accessibility modes](/foundations/accessibility-modes/).

## Density

`data-pu-density="compact"`, `"comfortable"` (the default) or `"spacious"` on any element resizes control and field heights (`--pu-control-*`), table cell padding (`--pu-cell-x`, `--pu-cell-y`) and row heights (`--pu-row-min`, `--pu-row-lg`) below it. Use compact for trading screens and dense blotters, comfortable for mobile banking, spacious for touch kiosks and low-vision users.

Density changes what you see, not what you can hit. Compact controls stay at 28 px or more (WCAG 2.5.8 asks for 24), buttons grow their hit area to 44 px on touch screens, and on a coarse pointer compact fields keep the comfortable heights.

Pick theme, contrast and density together:

```
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { useLocalStorage } from '@punji/ui/hooks';
import { themeAttributes, type Density } from '@punji/ui/theme';

interface Modes {
  theme: 'system' | 'light' | 'dark';
  contrast: 'normal' | 'more';
  density: Density;
}

const isModes = (v: unknown): v is Modes => typeof v === 'object' && v !== null && 'theme' in v && 'contrast' in v && 'density' in v;
const options = { defaultValue: { theme: 'system', contrast: 'normal', density: 'comfortable' } as Modes, validate: isModes };

/** Applies the saved theme, contrast and density to <html>. "system" and "comfortable" remove their attribute. */
export function useModes() {
  const { value, set } = useLocalStorage<Modes>('app-modes', options);
  useEffect(() => {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    const attrs = themeAttributes(value);
    for (const name of ['data-theme', 'data-pu-density'] as const) {
      const next = attrs[name];
      if (next) root.setAttribute(name, next);
      else root.removeAttribute(name);
    }
  }, [value]);
  return [value, set] as const;
}
```

## Brand theming with createTheme

`createTheme` from `@punji/ui/theme` turns a brand into CSS for the `punji.tokens` layer: accent, accent fill and its hover, the text on the fill, link and chart colours for light and dark, plus control and card radii and fonts. Missing values are derived (the text on the fill is black or white, whichever contrasts more). Every colour is checked against the library surfaces when the CSS is generated, and misses come back as `issues` (or a thrown error with `strict: true`). Values must be hex colours, lengths, font lists and a plain selector, so untrusted input cannot break out of a declaration.

Gain, loss, debit and credit are not brand colours. They come from the library defaults or from a vetted `finance` palette: `green-up`, `red-up` for markets that show a rise in red, or `blue-orange`, which stays distinct for red-green colour blindness. The high-contrast themes ignore the brand, so they keep their ratios. Generate the CSS at build time and load it after the package stylesheet.

theme.ts: a bank brand, generated at build time:

```
// Run at build time (a script, or a server route), then serve the CSS after @punji/ui/styles.css.
import { createTheme } from '@punji/ui/theme';

const brand = createTheme(
  {
    light: { accent: '#0b5cad', accentFill: '#0b5cad' },
    dark: { accent: '#8bbcff', accentFill: '#8bbcff' },
    radius: { control: '8px', card: '16px' },
    font: { body: '"Inter", system-ui, sans-serif' },
    // Gain and loss stay independent of the brand blue.
    finance: 'green-up',
  },
  { strict: true },
);

// brand.css starts with: @layer punji.tokens { :root, [data-theme='light'] { --pu-color-accent: #0b5cad; ... } }
// brand.issues lists any colour that misses 4.5:1 (or 3:1 for the chart); strict throws instead.
export const brandCss: string = brand.css;
```

## Right to left

Set `dir="rtl"` on `<html>` or a section. Component CSS uses logical properties (`padding-inline-start`, `inset-inline-end`, `text-align: end`), so layout, borders, popovers, pinned table columns and progress fills mirror. Chevrons and arrows that point along the line flip; the switch thumb, sliders, meters and column resizing follow the direction. Amounts, prices and changes stay an isolated left-to-right run, so the sign and currency keep their place. Charts keep time running left to right.

## Override tokens

To change a token everywhere, set it on `:root` in your own CSS. Because your rule is unlayered it wins; set it for both themes if the value is colour, since light and dark have their own values.

app.css: a house radius and a calmer link colour:

```
:root {
  --pu-radius-control: 8px;
}

:root,
[data-theme='light'] {
  --pu-color-link: #1f4f8f;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme='light']) {
    --pu-color-link: #9cc2ff;
  }
}

[data-theme='dark'] {
  --pu-color-link: #9cc2ff;
}
```

## Override the accent for one section

The library ships one theme. A host application that has its own brand or per-area accent (for example a public site in green and an account area in lime) does not need a second theme: it scopes the accent tokens to the area. That is the documented extension point and the project's architecture decision.

Put a data attribute on the section, override `--pu-color-accent` with its related tokens, and give both themes a value. Check the contrast of the values you choose: link and accent text need 4.5 to 1 against the surface, and the text on the accent fill needs the same.

app.css: scope the accent to data-section="research":

```
[data-section='research'] {
  --pu-color-accent: #0b5cad;
  --pu-color-link: #0b5cad;
  --pu-color-accent-fill: #8bbcff;
  --pu-color-accent-fill-hover: #a3cbff;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme='light']) [data-section='research'] {
    --pu-color-accent: #8bbcff;
    --pu-color-link: #8bbcff;
  }
}

[data-theme='dark'] [data-section='research'] {
  --pu-color-accent: #8bbcff;
  --pu-color-link: #8bbcff;
}
```

The section:

```
import type { ReactNode } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@punji/ui/button';
import { Card } from '@punji/ui/card';

// The section carries a data attribute; app.css (see the CSS block) re-points the accent tokens inside it.
// Everything built on those tokens (links, the primary button, charts) follows, with no change to the components.
export function ResearchSection({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <section data-section="research">
      <Card title="Research" description="Accent overridden for this section only" footer={<Button size="sm">Open report</Button>}>
        {children}
      </Card>
    </section>
  );
}
```

## Rules

What keeps a theme safe.

- Override tokens, not component classes. Class names are internal and can change; tokens are the contract.
- Do not copy hex values into your own CSS: read `var(--pu-color-text)` so your screens follow the theme.
- Gain and loss colours are never the only signal in components; keep that when you build your own.
- Text tokens are checked for contrast against their surfaces in all four themes by `scripts/check-contrast.mjs`. Hand-written overrides are not; `createTheme` checks the colours it writes.

## Example: Theme, contrast, density and direction

One panel, four attributes. Everything inside follows them.

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@punji/ui/button';
import { SegmentedControl } from '@punji/ui/controls';
import { Field } from '@punji/ui/fields';
import { Delta } from '@punji/ui/figures';
import { MoneyDisplay } from '@punji/ui/money-display';
import { Switch } from '@punji/ui/switch';
import { FinancialTable } from '@punji/ui/financial';
import { themeAttributes, type Density } from '@punji/ui/theme';

type Scheme = 'light' | 'dark';
type Contrast = 'normal' | 'more';
type Dir = 'ltr' | 'rtl';

const rows = [
  { symbol: 'NABIL', units: 120, change: 2.41 },
  { symbol: 'NTC', units: 40, change: -1.18 },
  { symbol: 'HDL', units: 15, change: 0 },
];

/** Theme, contrast, density and direction applied to one preview panel with themeAttributes. */
export default function Example() {
  const [scheme, setScheme] = useState<Scheme>('light');
  const [contrast, setContrast] = useState<Contrast>('normal');
  const [density, setDensity] = useState<Density>('comfortable');
  const [dir, setDir] = useState<Dir>('ltr');
  const [alerts, setAlerts] = useState(true);
  const [symbol, setSymbol] = useState('NABIL');
  const attrs = themeAttributes({ theme: scheme, contrast, density });
  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'grid', gap: 16 }}>
      <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: 12 }}>
        <SegmentedControl<Scheme> label="Theme" prefix="mode-theme" size="sm" value={scheme} onChange={setScheme} options={[{ value: 'light', label: 'Light' }, { value: 'dark', label: 'Dark' }]} />
        <SegmentedControl<Contrast> label="Contrast" prefix="mode-contrast" size="sm" value={contrast} onChange={setContrast} options={[{ value: 'normal', label: 'Standard' }, { value: 'more', label: 'High' }]} />
        <SegmentedControl<Density> label="Density" prefix="mode-density" size="sm" value={density} onChange={setDensity} options={[{ value: 'compact', label: 'Compact' }, { value: 'comfortable', label: 'Comfortable' }, { value: 'spacious', label: 'Spacious' }]} />
        <SegmentedControl<Dir> label="Direction" prefix="mode-dir" size="sm" value={dir} onChange={setDir} options={[{ value: 'ltr', label: 'LTR' }, { value: 'rtl', label: 'RTL' }]} />
      </div>
      <div {...attrs} dir={dir} style={{ display: 'grid', gap: 12, padding: 'var(--pu-space-4)', borderRadius: 'var(--pu-radius-lg)', border: '1px solid var(--pu-color-card-border)', background: 'var(--pu-color-bg)', color: 'var(--pu-color-text)' }}>
        <code style={{ fontSize: 12, color: 'var(--pu-color-text-muted)' }}>{Object.entries({ ...attrs, dir }).map(([k, v]) => `${k}="${v}"`).join(' ')}</code>
        <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexWrap: 'wrap', alignItems: 'end', gap: 12 }}>
          <div style={{ flex: '1 1 12rem' }}>
            <Field label="Symbol" value={symbol} onChange={setSymbol} />
          </div>
          <Button>Buy</Button>
          <Button variant="secondary">Sell</Button>
        </div>
        <span style={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: 8 }}>
          <Switch checked={alerts} onChange={setAlerts} label="Price alerts" /> Price alerts
        </span>
        <p style={{ margin: 0 }}>
          Cash <MoneyDisplay value={-4875050n} currency="NPR" locale="en-IN" />
        </p>
        <FinancialTable
          caption="Holdings"
          rows={rows}
          rowKey={(r) => r.symbol}
          emptyTitle="No holdings"
          columns={[
            { key: 'symbol', label: 'Symbol', rowHeader: true, render: (r) => r.symbol },
            { key: 'units', label: 'Units', num: true, render: (r) => String(r.units) },
            { key: 'change', label: 'Change', num: true, render: (r) => <Delta value={r.change} kind="pct" /> },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```


