# Theming, contrast, density and RTL

Every component reads `--pu-*` custom properties from the `punji.tokens`
cascade layer ([tokens.md](tokens.md)). Four things change them, each an
attribute on any element, so a page, a section or one panel can differ:

| What | Attribute | Values | CSS |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Colour theme | `data-theme` | none (follow the system), `light`, `dark`, `light-hc`, `dark-hc` | `tokens.css`, `css/contrast.css` |
| Density | `data-pu-density` | `compact`, `comfortable` (default), `spacious` | `tokens.css` |
| Direction | `dir` | `ltr`, `rtl` | component CSS (logical properties), `base.css` |
| Forced colours | none: the OS turns it on | `forced-colors: active` | `css/contrast.css` |

`@punji/ui/styles.css` has all of it. With granular CSS, add
`@punji/ui/css/contrast.css` after `tokens.css` for the high-contrast themes
and forced-colours rules.

The live switcher is on the docs site: [Theming](https://design.punji.app/get-started/theming/)
and [Accessibility modes](https://design.punji.app/foundations/accessibility-modes/).

## Choosing modes from a setting

`themeAttributes` from `@punji/ui/theme` (pure, no React) maps a user setting
to the attributes. `system` and `comfortable` leave their attribute off.

```ts
import { themeAttributes } from '@punji/ui/theme';

themeAttributes({ theme: 'dark', contrast: 'more', density: 'compact' });
// { 'data-theme': 'dark-hc', 'data-pu-density': 'compact' }
```

Spread the result on an element (`<div {...attrs}>`) or copy it onto
`document.documentElement` in an effect. Set the attributes before first
paint to avoid a flash; see the SSR guide on the site.

## High contrast

`light-hc` and `dark-hc` set every colour token. They meet WCAG 2.2 AAA:

- Text tokens: 7:1 or more on `bg`, `surface` and `surface-tint` (1.4.6).
- Control borders, the chart colour and series: 4.5:1 or more (stricter than
  the 3:1 of 1.4.11).
- Borders and dividers are solid, shadows become 2 px outlines, focus rings
  are 3 px.

With no `data-theme` on `<html>`, the page switches to them when the system
asks for more contrast (`prefers-contrast: more`, for example macOS
"Increase contrast"). An explicit `data-theme` always wins.

### The contrast gate

`scripts/check-contrast.mjs` (`npm run check:contrast`, and part of
`npm run lint`) reads the token blocks, composites translucent colours over
their surface, and checks 41 pairs in all four themes. It also fails when the
`prefers-color-scheme` dark block drifts from `[data-theme='dark']`, when a
high-contrast theme leaves a colour unset, or when the `prefers-contrast`
blocks differ from their theme. `--json` prints every ratio.

| Pair | light | dark | light-hc | dark-hc |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `text` on `surface` | 17.43 | 15.95 | 21.00 | 21.00 |
| `text-muted` on `surface` | 7.26 | 7.36 | 16.48 | 16.83 |
| `text-tertiary` on `bg` | 5.38 | 5.93 | 13.58 | 14.45 |
| `link` on `surface` | 6.01 | 13.61 | 12.69 | 18.31 |
| `gain` on `surface-tint` | 5.48 | 12.34 | 8.72 | 15.18 |
| `loss` on `surface-tint` | 5.92 | 6.96 | 8.19 | 10.15 |
| `warn` on `surface-tint` | 5.80 | 9.00 | 8.86 | 12.23 |
| `on-accent-fill` on `accent-fill` | 14.92 | 14.92 | 12.69 | 18.31 |
| `text-inverse` on `surface-inverse` | 15.95 | 16.83 | 21.00 | 21.00 |
| `border-control` on `surface-tint` | 3.53 | 3.40 | 18.43 | 17.40 |
| `chart` on `surface` | 3.76 | 13.61 | 9.93 | 18.31 |

Lowest text ratio per theme: light 5.13 (`gain` on `bg`), dark 4.90
(`text-tertiary` on `surface-tint`), light-hc 8.09, dark-hc 9.33. The
standard themes target AA (4.5:1), the `-hc` themes AAA (7:1).

## Forced colours (Windows High Contrast)

The OS replaces author colours with a small system palette and removes
backgrounds and shadows. `css/contrast.css` redraws what would otherwise
vanish, using system colours only:

- Focus: a 2 px `Highlight` outline everywhere, including inputs whose ring
  is normally a box-shadow on the wrapper.
- Edges: buttons, chips, segmented controls, cards, popovers, drawers,
  option lists and tooltips keep a `CanvasText` border.
- Selected, pressed and current (tabs, segmented controls, chips, options,
  account and keypad chips, date presets, shell navigation): `Highlight`
  background with `HighlightText`. Checkboxes, table selection boxes and
  switches are redrawn with `forced-color-adjust: none`.
- Data fills: gain bars solid, loss bars hatched, extra series striped.
- Charts: SVG paint is not forced, so marks are drawn in `CanvasText`. Up
  candles are hollow and down candles filled; the second, third and fourth
  series are dashed, dotted and dash-dotted; depth asks are dashed; loss
  sparklines are dashed.
- Gain and loss text keeps its sign (`+`, `−`) and arrow (`▲`, `▼`) in every
  mode, so colour is never the only signal.

## Density

`data-pu-density` sets these tokens for everything below the element. They
are kept out of the colour theme blocks, so a nested `data-theme` does not
reset density.

| Token | compact | comfortable | spacious | Read by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `--pu-control-sm` | 28px | 32px | 36px | small buttons, segmented controls |
| `--pu-control-md` | 32px | 36px | 44px | buttons, `md` fields, steppers |
| `--pu-control-lg` | 36px | 44px | 52px | fields, large buttons |
| `--pu-cell-y` / `--pu-cell-x` | 5px / 8px | 10px / 12px | 14px / 16px | table cells, amortisation and rate tables |
| `--pu-row-min` | 36px | 48px | 56px | `DataRow` |
| `--pu-row-lg` | 56px | 72px | 80px | `ValueRow` |

Use compact for trading screens, order blotters and dense ledgers;
comfortable for mobile banking; spacious for kiosks and low-vision users.

Touch targets: compact never goes below 28 px (WCAG 2.5.8 asks for 24 px).
Buttons extend their hit area to `--pu-tap-min` (44 px) on coarse pointers,
and on a coarse pointer compact keeps the comfortable field heights
(44 px and 36 px). Components with their own `density` prop (`StockTable`,
`HoldingsTable`) keep it; the attribute changes the shared tokens.

## Gain and loss colour convention

One setting decides which colour means a rise: the theme. Components read `--pu-color-gain` and `--pu-color-loss` and
nothing else, so `createTheme({ ..., finance: 'red-up' })` makes every candlestick, performance, depth, column and heat
chart, `PriceChange`, table and breadth bar red-up exactly once. Use it for markets that show a rise in red (China, Japan,
South Korea, Taiwan); keep `green-up` for most others, and offer `blue-orange` for red-green colour blindness. The theme
also writes `--pu-finance-convention` (absent means `green-up`).

The per-component `convention` prop (`'green-up' | 'red-up'`) is an **absolute per-instance override**, for a dashboard
that mixes markets. It points that component's gain and loss at the fixed `--pu-hue-green` and `--pu-hue-red`, so it never
swaps a second time on top of a red-up theme: `convention="red-up"` is red-up in any theme, and leaving it off follows the
theme. Prefer the theme; reach for the prop only when one widget must differ. (Under `blue-orange` the hues are blue and
orange, so the prop re-points to those.)

Never rely on colour alone: every figure also carries a sign, arrow or word, and gain and loss differ in lightness as well
as hue (at least 1.4:1, asserted by `scripts/check-contrast.mjs`).

## Radius, spacing and type scales

Radius (`--pu-radius-4` to `-24`, plus `-card` and `-control`), spacing (`--pu-space-*`, 4px grid) and type (`--pu-font-weight-*`, `--pu-line-height-*`, `--pu-font-numeric`) are tokens you can override like colours. `--pu-radius-sm/md/lg/xl/2xl/3xl` are deprecated and no longer read by components; see the scales section of [tokens.md](tokens.md).

## Brand theming

`createTheme` from `@punji/ui/theme` builds a typed, validated brand:

```ts
import { createTheme } from '@punji/ui/theme';

const { css, issues, vars } = createTheme(
  {
    light: { accent: '#0b5cad' },                 // accentFill, hover, onAccentFill, link, chart are derived
    dark: { accent: '#8bbcff' },
    radius: { control: '8px', card: '16px' },
    font: { body: '"Inter", system-ui, sans-serif' },
    finance: 'green-up',                          // or 'red-up', 'blue-orange'
  },
  { selector: ':root', strict: false },           // or "[data-brand='acme']" to scope it
);
```

- **Output:** CSS for the `punji.tokens` layer with the same selector shapes
  as `tokens.css`, so it wins when loaded after the package stylesheet.
  Generate it at build time and serve it as a file.
- **Contrast on generation:** accent and link need 4.5:1 on `bg`, `surface`
  and `surface-tint`; the text on the fill needs 4.5:1 on the fill and its
  hover; the chart colour needs 3:1 on `surface`. Misses are returned in
  `issues` (`{ theme, token, against, ratio, min }`); `strict: true` throws a
  `RangeError` instead. Missing values are derived: the fill is the accent,
  the hover moves 10% toward black (light) or white (dark), the text on the
  fill is black or white, whichever contrasts more.
- **Finance semantics stay out of the brand.** There is no brand key for gain,
  loss, debit or credit. `finance` picks a vetted palette: `green-up` (the
  default, no override), `red-up` (a rise in red, for markets that use it;
  every chart, `PriceChange` and table follows it once; see
  [Gain and loss colour convention](#gain-and-loss-colour-convention)), or
  `blue-orange` (distinct for red-green colour blindness). Each passes 4.5:1
  on every surface. `financePalette(name)` returns the colours.
- **High contrast is never branded.** At `:root` the `-hc` themes win on
  specificity and the system dark block skips `prefers-contrast: more`; a
  scoped brand skips sections inside a `-hc` theme and steps aside under
  `prefers-contrast: more`.
- **Safe input:** colours must be `#rgb` or `#rrggbb`, radii a length
  (`px`, `rem`, `em`), fonts only names, quotes, commas and hyphens, and the
  selector plain (no braces, semicolons, comments or angle brackets). Anything
  else throws a `TypeError`, so a brand read from a database cannot inject
  CSS.

`contrastRatio(a, b)` is exported for your own checks.

### Without JavaScript

The CSS recipe is the same shape by hand: set the brand tokens for both
themes in your own stylesheet after the package CSS (see the "Override
tokens" and "Override the accent for one section" sections of the Theming
guide), and check each colour with `contrastRatio` or any WCAG checker.

## Right to left

- Component CSS uses logical properties (`margin-inline-start`,
  `inset-inline-end`, `padding-inline`, `border-start-start-radius`,
  `text-align: start | end`), so layout, borders, popovers, option lists,
  pinned table columns, progress fills and meters mirror under `dir="rtl"`.
- Chevrons and arrows that point along the line (`ChevronRight`,
  `ChevronLeft`, pagination, `CheckRow`, `DrawerHeader` back, `CompanySearch`
  back, `NetBarHead` arrows) carry `pu-flip` and mirror.
- The `Switch` thumb and the drawer entrance move with `--pu-dir` (1 or -1,
  set by `dir`). `RangeSlider`'s fill, `Meter` and `RangeBar` marks use
  logical insets, so they line up with the native inputs.
- `VirtualFinancialTable` pins with `insetInlineStart` / `insetInlineEnd`;
  the pinned edge shadow flips, and drag-resizing inverts its direction.
- Amounts and changes (`MoneyDisplay`, `Figure`, `Delta`, `PriceChange`,
  figure chips) render as an isolated left-to-right run, so the sign,
  digits and currency keep their order inside Arabic or Hebrew text.
- Charts (`CandlestickChart`, `PerformanceChart`, `DepthChart`,
  `ColumnChart`) keep time running left to right; their plot area is `ltr`.
- Not mirrored on purpose: checkmarks, spinners, the down chevron of selects
  and "show more".

The visual fixtures `rtl` and `theme-modes` (`test/visual/fixtures-rtl.tsx`)
draw these pieces in RTL beside LTR, and every density and high-contrast
theme. Their baselines are pending (`npm run test:visual:update` in Docker).

## Known gaps

- `VirtualFinancialTable` row height is a JS prop (`rowHeight`), so density
  does not change it; pass a smaller `rowHeight` for compact screens.
- Physical 4-value shorthands inside a few component-specific rules were
  converted automatically; components with JS-positioned overlays (`InfoTip`,
  `Tooltip`, chart tooltips) position in physical pixels, which is correct
  for fixed and LTR-plot overlays.
- No manual Windows High Contrast or screen-reader pass yet; the forced-colours
  rules are checked by unit tests and the fixtures, not on a Windows machine.

guides.router:
# Using with a router

> Wrap your router link once with LinkProvider and every Punji link, button and row routes through it: React Router, Next.js Link and TanStack Router.

URL: https://design.punji.app/get-started/router/
Section: Get started (get-started)

Punji has no router dependency. Components that take an `href` render a plain `<a>` unless you give them a link component. Wrap your router's link once in `LinkProvider` and Button, CardLink, StatusPill, CheckRow, ValueRow, LinkTable and PageHeader all use it for in-app paths. A `linkComponent` prop on one component still wins, and absolute web, mailto and tel addresses always stay a plain `<a>`.

## The link contract

A link component receives `href` (always a string path), `children`, `className`, `target`, `rel`, `aria-*` attributes, `data-testid`, `onClick` and a `ref`. React 19 passes `ref` as an ordinary prop, so a wrapper only has to spread what it gets. The type is `LinkComponent`, exported from `@punji/ui/link` and the root.

Which link is used, in order: the component's own `linkComponent` prop, then the nearest `LinkProvider`, then a plain `<a>`. Nothing is renamed or removed: code that passes `linkComponent` today keeps working.

## React Router

React Router's `Link` takes `to` where Punji gives `href`, so map one onto the other. Put the provider inside the router.

app.tsx (React Router):

```
import { Link } from 'react-router';
import { LinkProvider } from '@punji/ui/link';
import type { LinkComponent } from '@punji/ui/link';

const RouterLink: LinkComponent = ({ href, ...rest }) => <Link to={href} {...rest} />;

export function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <LinkProvider component={RouterLink}>{children}</LinkProvider>;
}
```

## Next.js

`next/link` accepts `href` directly, with `prefetch` and `replace` if you want them on every link. The provider is a client component, so put it in a small `'use client'` file and render it in the root layout (see [Next.js App Router](/get-started/nextjs/)).

app/providers.tsx (Next.js):

```
'use client';

import Link from 'next/link';
import { LinkProvider } from '@punji/ui/link';
import type { LinkComponent } from '@punji/ui/link';

const NextLink: LinkComponent = ({ href, ...rest }) => <Link href={href} {...rest} />;

export function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <LinkProvider component={NextLink}>{children}</LinkProvider>;
}
```

## TanStack Router

TanStack Router's `Link` takes `to`. Its types are strict about known routes, so the wrapper accepts any string path and casts once.

providers.tsx (TanStack Router):

```
import { Link } from '@tanstack/react-router';
import { LinkProvider } from '@punji/ui/link';
import type { LinkComponent } from '@punji/ui/link';

const RouterLink: LinkComponent = ({ href, ...rest }) => <Link to={href as string} {...rest} />;

export function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <LinkProvider component={RouterLink}>{children}</LinkProvider>;
}
```

## Which addresses are blocked

Every `href` goes through `safeHref` before it reaches a plain `<a>` or your link component. Relative paths, `#fragments`, `?queries`, `http:`, `https:`, `mailto:` and `tel:` pass. Any other scheme, including `javascript:`, `vbscript:` and `data:` (also with capitals, spaces or line breaks inside the scheme), is blocked: the component renders an `<a>` with no `href` and `aria-disabled="true"`, your link component is never called, and in development a warning names the scheme. Empty addresses are blocked the same way.

Absolute `http(s)`, `mailto:` and `tel:` addresses always render a plain `<a>`, never the router link. A link with `target="_blank"` always gets `rel="noopener noreferrer"`, also through your link component.

Call `safeHref(value)` yourself when you build your own anchors from data: it returns the address, or `undefined` when it is blocked.

guides.tables:
# Searchable and paginated tables

`DataTable` keeps native table semantics and provides optional controlled search, selection and page controls. `FinancialTable` passes the same controls through while rendering typed financial rows. Neither component fetches data or guesses which business fields are safe to search.

```tsx
const matching = rows
  .filter((row) => matches(row, query))
  .toSorted(compareRows);
const pageRows = matching.slice(pageIndex * pageSize, (pageIndex + 1) * pageSize);

<FinancialTable
  caption="Holdings · NPR"
  columns={columns}
  rows={pageRows}
  rowKey={(row) => row.symbol}
  emptyTitle="No holdings match your search."
  search={{
    label: 'Search holdings',
    placeholder: 'Symbol or company',
    value: query,
    onChange: setQuery,
  }}
  pagination={{
    pageIndex,
    pageSize,
    rowCount: matching.length,
    onPageChange: setPageIndex,
  }}
/>
```

Selection is also controlled. Give each `DataListRow` a stable domain ID; select-page changes only the visible rows and keeps selections from other pages or filters:

```tsx
<DataTable
  caption="Open invoices"
  columns={columns}
  selection={{
    selectedRowIds,
    onChange: setSelectedRowIds,
    rowLabel: (id) => `Select invoice ${id}`,
  }}
>
  {pageRows.map((invoice) => (
    <DataListRow key={invoice.id} rowId={invoice.id}>
      {/* cells */}
    </DataListRow>
  ))}
</DataTable>
```

## Data contract

- Search, sorting and pagination operate in that order over the complete client data set: filter first, sort the matches, then slice the requested page. Pass the filtered total as `rowCount`; never pass only the current page length.
- `pageIndex` is zero-based and `pageSize` is a positive integer. Display labels are one-based. Changing the search value sends page `0` through `onPageChange` when the current page is later than the first.
- These props control the search input and page buttons. The caller owns row filtering, sorting, network requests, and the current row set. This supports local data, server pagination and URL-backed state without making the component infer financial semantics.
- For server-side data, apply the query and sort on the server, request the requested page, and pass the filtered server total in `rowCount`. A search over only the currently loaded page can produce false “no match” results.
- Selection IDs must be stable domain identifiers, never visible row positions or page offsets. IDs stay in controlled component state and are not written to row DOM attributes; provide a user-facing `rowLabel` rather than a private key. The header checkbox selects or clears only selectable rows on the current page; individual IDs already selected elsewhere remain selected. `selectionDisabled` on `DataListRow` excludes that row from selection. Filtering a row out of view does not silently discard its selected ID.
- If the current data set has no rows, `empty` is shown inside the table and the search remains available. `loading` keeps the column headers and marks the table region busy. `FinancialTable` also keeps its established full-page loading and error states.

## Accessibility and safe rendering

The search field and row selection checkboxes have accessible names, page buttons sit in a caption-named navigation region, and the selection checkbox announces partial selection. Stacked phone tables expose a visible select-page control outside the clipped header. Selection count and range updates are announced politely. Table captions and column headers remain semantic, including when the visual header is clipped by the mobile stack layout.

Render values as React nodes; the components do not use HTML injection. Keep search accessors explicit in the caller when data includes private or sensitive fields. Validate selected IDs and permissions on the server before bulk operations. Punji UI does not log queries or row data. Client-side permissions are not a security boundary.

Import the aggregate stylesheet once:

```ts
import '@punji/ui/styles.css';
```

The individual table stylesheet is `@punji/ui/css/table.css`; it needs `tokens.css` and `base.css` as described in [Using Punji UI](../README.md#using-the-library).


## Large tables: VirtualFinancialTable

For thousands of rows (a floorsheet, a trade blotter, statement lines), `VirtualFinancialTable` from `@punji/ui/virtual-table` takes the same typed columns as `FinancialTable` but draws only the rows in view. It has no pagination: pass every filtered, sorted row.

```tsx
import { VirtualFinancialTable, compareByRules, type SortRule } from '@punji/ui/virtual-table';
import { TableExport, rowsWithIds } from '@punji/ui/table-export';
import '@punji/ui/css/table.css';
import '@punji/ui/css/virtual-table.css';

const rowKey = (t: Trade) => t.id; // stable: keys are recomputed when it changes
const [sort, setSort] = useState<SortRule[]>([{ key: 'time', dir: 'desc' }]);
const rows = useMemo(() => trades.filter(matches).toSorted(compareByRules(sort, accessors)), [trades, sort, query]);

<VirtualFinancialTable
  caption="Floorsheet · NPR"
  columns={columns}            // { key, label, render, num?, rowHeader?, sortable?, width?, minWidth?, maxWidth?, pin?: 'left' | 'right', resizable? }
  rows={rows}
  rowKey={rowKey}
  emptyTitle="No trades match"
  height={480}                 // the scroll box's maximum height
  rowHeight={40}               // fixed; `measureRows` measures drawn rows instead
  sort={sort}
  onSortChange={setSort}       // Shift-click or Shift+Enter adds a column
  columnWidths={widths}        // controlled widths, e.g. from useLocalStorage
  onColumnResize={(key, width) => setWidths((w) => ({ ...w, [key]: width }))}
  selection={{ selectedRowIds, onChange: setSelectedRowIds, rowLabel: (id) => `Select trade ${id}` }}
  toolbar={<TableExport columns={csvColumns} filename="floorsheet" visibleRows={rows} allRows={trades} selectedRows={rowsWithIds(rows, selectedRowIds, rowKey)} />}
/>
```

- **Virtualisation**: the window plus `overscan` rows (default 8) are drawn; spacer rows hold the height. `aria-rowcount` on the table and `aria-rowindex` on each row give screen readers the true size and position. The focused row stays drawn when it scrolls away, so focus is never lost. Server rendering draws the first screenful from `height` and `rowHeight`, so hydration matches.
- **Keyboard**: one row is in the tab order; Up and Down move between rows, Page Up and Page Down jump a screen, Home and End go to the first and last row; Space toggles selection. Sort heads are buttons; resize handles are focusable separators (Left/Right 10 px, Shift 40 px, Home/End to the bounds).
- **Pinning**: `pin: 'left'` columns come first and `pin: 'right'` last, both sticky while the table scrolls sideways. The selection column is always pinned left.
- **Resizing**: pointer drag on a head's right edge or the keyboard; widths are clamped to `minWidth` (default 64) and `maxWidth` (default 560). `onColumnResize` fires when a drag ends and on every key, so persisting there is cheap. Without `columnWidths` the table keeps widths itself.
- **Multi-sort**: `nextSortRules` is the transition the heads use (plain press: only this column, flipping when it already was; Shift: add ascending, then descending, then remove). The primary column carries `aria-sort`; with more than one rule each sorted head shows its priority number and reads its direction and priority, and every change is announced. `compareByRules` sorts with missing values last.
- **Live rows**: pass a new `rows` array per batch, ideally once per animation frame, replacing only the changed row objects. Rows inserted or removed above the view keep the reader's rows in place; a re-sort keeps the scroll offset.
- **Export**: `TableExport` writes CSV through `toCsv` with formula neutralisation always on (it cannot be turned off here) and a UTF-8 BOM by default, from the rows you pass for each scope. It runs in the browser only; nothing is sent anywhere. `allRows` should hold only data the user may download.
- **Limits**: columns are not virtualised (all columns render for drawn rows); `wide` and `hideUnder` do not apply (use pinning and horizontal scroll); there is no stacked phone layout; browsers cap element height (about 33 million px in Chromium), so at 40 px rows the practical ceiling is about 800,000 rows.

Measured numbers and the benchmark: [PERFORMANCE.md](PERFORMANCE.md).


